MD["how_val"] = """**How the measurement is validated.** The gold set is 450 items (180 national headlines, 120 local headlines, 150 Reddit posts; a third each from 2010 to 2019, 2020 to 2024 and 2025 to 2026), drawn on text features only. Two independent Claude Opus annotators (A, B) labelled it from the labelling guide alone; a third, fresh Opus instance adjudicated every item where A and B differed, seeing the two labels in random order without names. Agreement is Cohen's kappa (plus linear-weighted kappa and ordinal Krippendorff's alpha for tone, and Gwet's AC1 for skewed fields). The coder (Opus with `prompts/coder_v1.md`, items shown without date or outlet) is scored against the adjudicated gold labels by class, by source type, by period, separately on items where the annotators agreed or disagreed on that field, and on the 55 items with any disagreement. The baselines are the Loughran-McDonald dictionary with keyword rules, TF-IDF + logistic regression with 5-fold cross-validation on the gold labels, and the same classifier trained on Opus codes of non-gold items. These are LLM labels, not human labels, so agreement between Opus instances is an upper bound on reliability; the minimal-pair suite (labels known by construction), the test-retest run and the swap tests are the checks that do not lean on LLM labels."""

MD["how_fig1"] = """**How Figure 2 is made.** For each survey month t the economic data are the values published by day 14 of t (ALFRED vintages): the latest unemployment rate, headline PCE inflation over 12 months from the same vintage, and the latest quarter's real GDP growth (QoQ SAAR from the GDPC1 vintage). Model E is an OLS fit of the index on these three over Jan 2010 to Dec 2019. Panel (a) shows the fit and, from Jan 2020, the out-of-sample prediction with a 95% moving-block bootstrap band (block 12, coefficients re-estimated). Panel (b) is the gap (index minus prediction); grey bars are in-sample residuals, orange bars out-of-sample gaps. The COVID window (Mar 2020 to Jan 2021) is excluded by design because real-time GDP growth of -33 to +33% (annualised) drives a linear prediction off scale. Panel (c) repeats the model for the current and expectations indexes and for each of Michigan's five questions (their tables end in Aug 2026). Descriptive, except the bands."""

MD["how_rq1"] = """**How the bias measures are computed.** Item-level tests (selection, release days, calibration, attribution, groups) use only headlines the Opus coder judged relevant; the monthly Tier C series uses the relevance and tone of the TF-IDF classifier trained on Opus codes. A survey month's media window is the 15th of the previous month to the 14th, so it ends at the prediction date. Monthly news measures are the mean over four outlet groups (left-leaning national, center national, right-leaning national, local) of each group's share, with fixed equal weights, so nothing is estimated on later data. Tone = share negative minus share positive. The asymmetry test regresses monthly tone on the rise and the fall of unemployment (3-month change) and of inflation (12-month change in the 12-month rate) separately; the response to a deterioration is the coefficient on the rise, the response to an improvement minus the coefficient on the fall, and negativity bias means the first is larger (one-sided test). Release-day coverage is tone of headlines published on the day of and the day after each jobs or CPI release, grouped by how much the released number changed. Calibration compares each numerical claim with the official value in the data vintage of the publication date."""

MD["how_rq5"] = """**How the nowcasts are made.** On the prediction date of month t (day 14), each model is re-estimated on every earlier month (their sentiment values are all published by then) and applied to month t's real-time regressors. Model 1 uses the economy only, 1b adds the household-pressure controls, 2 adds the media measures to model 1, and 3 adds both. The Tier C media columns at origin t come from vintage t (scripts/09b): the classifier re-trained on codes published before day 15 of t and applied to every month up to t, so no code published after a nowcast's origin enters any value used at that origin. Accuracy is compared over the same months for every model. The Clark-West test asks whether the larger model's improvement is more than estimation noise would give (one-sided, HAC standard errors). The sensitivity grid swaps the media measure and the timing; its count of hits is compared with the count obtained after circularly shifting all media series by 13 to 57 months."""

